1
00:00:01,650 --> 00:00:05,396
When a heart stops, every second without compressions counts.

2
00:00:05,446 --> 00:00:06,562
This is Elvrek.

3
00:00:06,612 --> 00:00:12,240
A real-time safety controller for mechanical CPR, built on QNX 8.0.

4
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:21,038
Mechanical CPR machines never get tired, but their compressions, the breaths and the defibrillator usually run on separate clocks.

5
00:00:21,088 --> 00:00:25,425
Every extra second of pause before a shock lowers the odds of survival.

6
00:00:25,475 --> 00:00:30,209
Elvrek puts all three on one clock, ticking a thousand times a second.

7
00:00:30,259 --> 00:00:32,600
The AI only makes suggestions.

8
00:00:33,417 --> 00:00:34,471
Here is the cycle.

9
00:00:34,521 --> 00:00:37,199
The charger fills while the piston keeps working.

10
00:00:37,249 --> 00:00:45,059
The arm parks at full recoil only for the shock itself, a trusted check confirms the rhythm, and compressions resume on the next tick.

11
00:00:45,109 --> 00:00:48,497
The machine's part of that pause is about eight tenths of a second.

12
00:00:48,547 --> 00:00:50,817
The crew's time is counted separately.

13
00:00:51,617 --> 00:00:52,695
Why QNX?

14
00:00:52,745 --> 00:00:55,339
Every job here is its own small process.

15
00:00:55,389 --> 00:00:59,113
The safety process runs at the top priority on a timer pulse.

16
00:00:59,163 --> 00:01:04,918
The rhythm classifier runs unprivileged, and QNX refuses it the piston and the shock line.

17
00:01:04,968 --> 00:01:07,094
If it crashes, it is restarted.

18
00:01:07,144 --> 00:01:09,005
The safety process never is.

19
00:01:09,055 --> 00:01:11,777
A hardware watchdog lifts the cup instead.

20
00:01:12,283 --> 00:01:13,277
Now the real thing.

21
00:01:13,327 --> 00:01:18,806
QNX 8.0 boots in QEMU, with the whole stack built with qcc.

22
00:01:18,856 --> 00:01:24,933
The piston and chest are a physics model, behind the same interface a real motor driver will use.

23
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:27,938
First, the classifier attacks.

24
00:01:27,987 --> 00:01:32,168
It tries the piston, the shock line and every other forbidden path.

25
00:01:32,218 --> 00:01:35,450
Seven of seven are refused by the operating system.

26
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:37,993
Next, a shockable rhythm.

27
00:01:38,043 --> 00:01:40,421
Compressions run while the charger fills.

28
00:01:40,471 --> 00:01:46,587
The crew pre-authorises, the window opens, the shock fires, and compressions are back in under a second.

29
00:01:46,637 --> 00:01:52,460
Then carbon dioxide rises, a trusted sign of returning circulation, and the piston parks.

30
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:56,784
Last, we kill the safety process in the middle of a stroke.

31
00:01:56,834 --> 00:02:02,500
The driver's own watchdog lifts the cup to full recoil, and nothing restarts the controller.

32
00:02:02,983 --> 00:02:05,203
Every result is on the evidence page.

33
00:02:05,253 --> 00:02:09,482
Eleven of eleven fault cases pass on QNX, two runs in a row.

34
00:02:09,532 --> 00:02:14,794
It also shows what is not good enough yet, like our rhythm check on real arrest ECG.

35
00:02:14,844 --> 00:02:16,383
We say so plainly.

36
00:02:17,183 --> 00:02:23,213
You can replay a recorded run yourself, from the first compression to the moment circulation returns.

37
00:02:24,017 --> 00:02:28,147
Questions are answered in the FAQ, and the whole site works on a phone.

38
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:33,840
Elvrek is open source, and it is not a medical device.

39
00:02:33,890 --> 00:02:37,470
Next stop, a Raspberry Pi 5 and a training manikin.

40
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:39,350
Save lives, smarter.
